INT. SALT MINE CURLING HALL - NIGHT

A long gallery cut from white rock, lit by work lamps on cable. Painted lanes run down the salt floor. At the far end, a faded house.

The Deepwater Miners are down two ends in the final. Their last stone is about to go.

COACH DESMOND "DEZ" PRATT (50s, windbreaker, stopwatch on a lanyard) paces behind the hogline. Skip INEZ MOREAU (30s, braided, taped knuckles) crouches in the house with her broom flat on the ice, calling the line. At the hack, lead ABEBE TESFAYE (20s, rangy, headband) slides out of his delivery, the stone rumbling off, while Dez mouths numbers and Inez sweeps nothing, waiting. The stone curls, hesitates, and dies a foot short of the button.

Nobody speaks. Salt dust drifts in the lamplight.

DEZ
That's the game.

INEZ
It's not. We have one more.

DEZ
We had one more.

ABEBE
I pulled it. My fault.

INEZ
Your slide was fine. The ice was heavy.

Boots on the stairs. A man in a hard hat and reflective vest comes down the gallery, carrying a clipboard: FOREMAN GUS HALVERSEN (60s, soot in his creases, no smile). Dez straightens. Inez rises. Abebe stands the broom against the wall and wipes his palms on his knees.

GUS
Shift change in ten. You've got to clear the lane.

DEZ
Gus, we're mid-final.

GUS
You were mid-final.

INEZ
Give us the end. Please.

Gus looks at the stone, at the button, at the scoreboard. Taps his pencil on the clipboard.

GUS
One stone. Then you're out.

DEZ
Thank you.

GUS
Don't. Just throw it.

Abebe picks up the stone. His hands shake. Inez crosses to him and takes the handle for a second, then lets go.

INEZ
(softly)
Breathe out when you let go.

ABEBE
I know.

INEZ
I know you know.

He slides. It's a clean release. The stone goes long, slow, tired. It touches nothing.

The work lamps hum. Gus checks his watch.
